{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_27","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"nl -ba ARCH.md | sed -n '1,220p'\"","aggregated_output":"     1\t# Architecture\n     2\t\n     3\tLiving architecture document. Update when you change architecture.\n     4\t\n     5\t## Three-role agent model\n     6\t\n     7\tDarkfactory uses three agent roles, each running as a separate AI conversation:\n     8\t\n     9\t**Orchestrator** -- the main Claude Code session. Plans the work, dispatches one worker at a time, runs validators, manages fix loops and circuit breakers. Uses plan mode to keep instructions alive across long conversations. Defined by `skills/mission/SKILL.md` (10 phases, 0-9). In basic mode (`--basic`), the orchestrator also acts as the builder -- no workers are dispatched. Defined by `skills/mission/basic/SKILL.md`.\n    10\t\n    11\t**Workers** -- each builds a single task from a brief. Default engine is Codex (via `darkfactory-dispatch`); Claude is an alternative. A worker receives only its brief, never the contract. Makes changes in one commit and writes a handoff report.\n    12\t\n    13\t**Validators** -- check each worker's output. Two run back-to-back per task: scrutiny (build, test, lint, code review, contract check, execution check, test health) and user-test (tests the feature as a user would). Validators see the contract and the code, never the proposal or design.\n    14\t\n    15\t## Information walls\n    16\t\n    17\tWorkers never see the contract. Validators never see the design.\n    18\t\n    19\tThis forces validation to test what the code *does*, not what someone *intended*. Fix workers receive symptom descriptions, never the contract, so they solve real problems instead of gaming a checklist.\n    20\t\n    21\t## File-backed state\n    22\t\n    23\tAll state lives on disk, not in memory. Sessions can crash and resume. Progress is visible from a second terminal.\n    24\t\n    25\t```\n    26\topenspec/changes/<change-name>/\n    27\t  proposal.md              # What and why\n    28\t  specs/<cap>/spec.md      # WHEN/THEN requirements\n    29\t  design.md                # Architecture decisions, module inventory, type catalog\n    30\t  tasks.md                 # Ordered checklist with topology metadata\n    31\t  coverage-audit.md        # PRD-to-task traceability\n    32\t  contract.md              # Observable behavior checks, traced to specs\n    33\t  mission/\n    34\t    config.yaml            # Engine per role (codex or claude)\n    35\t    progress.md            # Running log\n    36\t    briefs/task-N.md       # What each worker receives\n    37\t    handoffs/task-N.md     # What each worker reports\n    38\t    validations/           # Validator reports\n    39\t    issues/                # Failure details per attempt\n    40\t    logs/                  # Streaming output (tail -f)\n    41\t```\n    42\t\n    43\t## Skill structure\n    44\t\n    45\tSkills live in `skills/` at the repo root. `install.sh` symlinks each into `~/.claude/skills/` for Claude Code discovery.\n    46\t\n    47\tEach skill is a self-contained module. Public interface: frontmatter (name, description). Implementation: SKILL.md body. No skill imports from another at the filesystem level.\n    48\t\n    49\tThe mission skill is intentionally monolithic (~900 lines). Companion files are read on demand:\n    50\t- `templates/` -- artifact formats (plan, contract, worker brief, design, etc.)\n    51\t- `prompts/` -- instructions handed to validator and reviewer subagents\n    52\t\n    53\t24 skills total: mission (with basic sub-skill), mission-resume, mission-status, mission-init, grill, language, prd, tdd, test-quality, security, adversarial, dispatch, artifact-review, lint-audit, skill-review, arch-review, domain-model, module-design, qa, write-a-skill, frontend, ui-system, react-perf.\n    54\t\n    55\tlint-audit audits a TypeScript project's oxlint config and tsconfig strictness against an agent-friendly ruleset. Part of the mechanical enforcement stack alongside the ts-quality.sh PostToolUse hook: the hook catches patterns at edit time (grep-based), the linter catches them at build time (AST-level via oxlint), and the compiler catches them at type-check time (tsconfig strictness). Gate 4 of the scrutiny validator runs the project's linter, so rules added by lint-audit are automatically enforced during missions.\n    56\t\n    57\t## Build loop\n    58\t\n    59\tFor each task: dispatch worker, run scrutiny validator, run user-test validator. On failure: diagnostic triage, then fix worker with symptoms only (information wall maintained). Circuit breaker after 3 attempts (1 build + 2 fixes) -- pauses for human decision.\n    60\t\n    61\tAfter task 1 passes, a foundation audit checks module structure and type names against the design.\n    62\t\n    63\t### Basic mode build loop\n    64\t\n    65\t`/mission --basic` is a simplified version of the full build loop designed for single-task work like bug fixes and small features. Instead of dispatching separate worker and validator agents, the orchestrator handles everything itself in one session.\n    66\t\n    67\tThe flow has 5 phases: orient (read the codebase, detect build/test tooling, generate a mini-contract of 3-8 expected behaviors), build (TDD by default, or direct implementation with `--no-tdd`), validate (5 sequential gates: build, test, lint, format, contract), fix (diagnose and retry on failure), and commit (single commit at the end).\n    68\t\n    69\tThe circuit breaker works the same way -- 3 total attempts before stopping -- but unlike the full loop, it doesn't pause to ask the user. It commits whatever it has and reports what failed. There are no information walls since there's only one agent doing all the work.\n    70\t\n    71\tDefined by `skills/mission/basic/SKILL.md`. The main `skills/mission/SKILL.md` routes to it when `--basic` is present in the arguments.\n    72\t\n    73\t## Planning constraints\n    74\t\n    75\tDesign phase scores 2-3 architecture candidates on a four-dimension rubric (separation of concerns, testability, task independence, module depth). A mandatory gate verifies the design includes an architecture decision, module inventory, type catalog, error strategy, integration points, and test strategy.\n    76\t\n    77\tBefore task generation, the orchestrator presents a decomposition strategy (layered, vertical, or hybrid) for human approval. Each task carries topology metadata: depends-on, consumes, produces, layers.\n    78\t\n    79\t## Configuration\n    80\t\n    81\tGlobal defaults in `~/.darkfactory/config.yaml`. Per-mission engine assignments in `mission/config.yaml` (worker, scrutiny_validator, usertest_validator, adversarial reviewers).\n    82\t\n    83\t## Versioning\n    84\t\n    85\tOne version for the whole repo: the GitHub release tag (v1.x). Internal tools (darkfactory-dispatch) have their own Cargo.toml version pinned at 1.0.0, which is never bumped. When releasing, increment the repo tag only.\n    86\t\n    87\t## External dependencies\n    88\t\n    89\t- Claude Code (orchestrator, Claude workers, validators)\n    90\t- Codex CLI (Codex workers, adversarial reviewer)\n    91\t- darkfactory-dispatch (Rust supervisor: process isolation, timeout, heartbeat, structured results)\n    92\t- OpenSpec CLI (planning artifact generation)\n    93\t- GitHub CLI (PRD issues, draft PRs)\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
