{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_35","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"nl -ba openspec/changes/lint-audit-skill/specs/ruleset-audit/spec.md | sed -n '1,240p'\"","aggregated_output":"     1\t## ADDED Requirements\n     2\t\n     3\t### Requirement: Compare oxlint rules against agent-friendly ruleset\n     4\tThe skill SHALL compare the project's oxlint configuration against the recommended agent-friendly ruleset defined in RULES.md.\n     5\t\n     6\t#### Scenario: Rule explicitly enabled\n     7\t- **WHEN** an agent-friendly rule (e.g., `typescript/no-explicit-any`) is set to `\"error\"` or `\"warn\"` in the oxlint config\n     8\t- **THEN** the skill reports it as `[x]` (enabled)\n     9\t\n    10\t#### Scenario: Rule missing from config\n    11\t- **WHEN** an agent-friendly rule is not present in the oxlint config\n    12\t- **THEN** the skill checks whether the rule is enabled by default in oxlint, and reports accordingly: `[x]` if default-enabled, `[ ]` if not\n    13\t\n    14\t#### Scenario: Rule explicitly disabled\n    15\t- **WHEN** an agent-friendly rule is set to `\"off\"` or `\"allow\"` in the oxlint config\n    16\t- **THEN** the skill reports it as `[!]` (explicitly disabled -- user chose to turn it off) with a note explaining why it matters for agents\n    17\t\n    18\t#### Scenario: No oxlint config (defaults only)\n    19\t- **WHEN** no oxlint config file exists\n    20\t- **THEN** the skill checks each agent-friendly rule against oxlint's default configuration and reports which are covered by defaults and which require explicit config\n    21\t\n    22\t### Requirement: Define RULES.md schema for agent-friendly ruleset\n    23\tThe RULES.md reference file SHALL use a structured format for each recommended rule.\n    24\t\n    25\t#### Scenario: Rule entry format\n    26\t- **WHEN** a rule is defined in RULES.md\n    27\t- **THEN** it includes: rule name (oxlint canonical), category, recommended severity (`error` or `warn`), whether it is enabled by default in oxlint, and a one-line rationale explaining why it matters for agents\n    28\t\n    29\t#### Scenario: Oxlint defaults pinned\n    30\t- **WHEN** RULES.md lists whether a rule is default-enabled\n    31\t- **THEN** the default status is pinned to a specific oxlint version noted at the top of RULES.md, so the audit script has a deterministic source of truth without shelling out\n    32\t\n    33\t### Requirement: Audit tsconfig strictness settings\n    34\tThe skill SHALL check each discovered tsconfig.json for recommended strictness settings.\n    35\t\n    36\t#### Scenario: strict mode enabled\n    37\t- **WHEN** `compilerOptions.strict` is `true` in the tsconfig\n    38\t- **THEN** the skill reports `strict` as `[x]` for that tsconfig\n    39\t\n    40\t#### Scenario: strict mode missing\n    41\t- **WHEN** `compilerOptions.strict` is not enabled in the effective tsconfig (resolved via `tsc --showConfig`)\n    42\t- **THEN** the skill reports `strict` as `[ ]` and runs `tsc -p <tsconfig> --strict --noEmit --pretty false 2>&1 | grep -c 'error TS'` to show migration scope\n    43\t\n    44\t#### Scenario: noUncheckedIndexedAccess enabled\n    45\t- **WHEN** `compilerOptions.noUncheckedIndexedAccess` is `true`\n    46\t- **THEN** the skill reports it as `[x]`\n    47\t\n    48\t#### Scenario: noUncheckedIndexedAccess missing\n    49\t- **WHEN** `compilerOptions.noUncheckedIndexedAccess` is not enabled in the effective tsconfig\n    50\t- **THEN** the skill reports it as `[ ]`, runs `tsc -p <tsconfig> --noUncheckedIndexedAccess --noEmit --pretty false 2>&1 | grep -c 'error TS'` for migration scope, and shows the recommendation \"Enables T | undefined for indexed access\"\n    51\t\n    52\t#### Scenario: erasableSyntaxOnly enabled\n    53\t- **WHEN** `compilerOptions.erasableSyntaxOnly` is `true`\n    54\t- **THEN** the skill reports it as `[x]`\n    55\t\n    56\t#### Scenario: erasableSyntaxOnly missing\n    57\t- **WHEN** `compilerOptions.erasableSyntaxOnly` is absent or `false`\n    58\t- **THEN** the skill reports it as `[ ]` with the recommendation \"Bans enum, namespace, parameter properties at compiler level\"\n    59\t\n    60\t### Requirement: Produce structured findings report\n    61\tThe skill SHALL produce a single combined report covering both oxlint rules and tsconfig settings.\n    62\t\n    63\t#### Scenario: Report format\n    64\t- **WHEN** the audit completes\n    65\t- **THEN** the report uses status markers (`[x]` enabled, `[ ]` missing, `[!]` partially/explicitly disabled) grouped under \"Oxlint Rules\" and \"TypeScript Compiler\" sections\n    66\t\n    67\t#### Scenario: Report includes migration scope\n    68\t- **WHEN** a tsconfig is missing `strict`\n    69\t- **THEN** the report shows the error count next to the recommendation: `[ ] strict (N errors to fix)`\n    70\t\n    71\t#### Scenario: Report includes rule rationale\n    72\t- **WHEN** a rule is reported as missing\n    73\t- **THEN** the report includes a one-line rationale from RULES.md explaining why the rule matters for agents\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"tool_end","dispatch_id":"01KSY2AH9DSVW6B8YQ47APVR24","ts":"2026-05-31T03:49:58Z","tool":"command_execution"}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_36","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"nl -ba openspec/changes/lint-audit-skill/specs/project-detection/spec.md | sed -n '1,260p'\"","aggregated_output":"     1\t## ADDED Requirements\n     2\t\n     3\t### Requirement: Detect project linter from package.json\n     4\tThe skill SHALL detect which linter the project uses by reading `devDependencies` and `dependencies` in `package.json`.\n     5\t\n     6\t#### Scenario: Oxlint project\n     7\t- **WHEN** `package.json` contains `oxlint` in `devDependencies` or `dependencies`\n     8\t- **THEN** the skill proceeds with the oxlint audit\n     9\t\n    10\t#### Scenario: ESLint-only project\n    11\t- **WHEN** `package.json` has a dependency with the exact package name `eslint` but not `oxlint`\n    12\t- **THEN** the skill reports \"ESLint-only project, skipping\" and exits without auditing. Packages like `eslint-plugin-react` or `@eslint/js` alone do not trigger the skip.\n    13\t\n    14\t#### Scenario: Both oxlint and ESLint present\n    15\t- **WHEN** `package.json` contains both `oxlint` and `eslint`\n    16\t- **THEN** the skill audits oxlint config and notes \"ESLint also present, auditing oxlint only\"\n    17\t\n    18\t#### Scenario: No linter detected\n    19\t- **WHEN** `package.json` contains neither `oxlint` nor `eslint`\n    20\t- **THEN** the skill reports \"No linter detected\" and shows the install command (`npm install -D oxlint`) without running it\n    21\t\n    22\t#### Scenario: No package.json\n    23\t- **WHEN** no `package.json` exists at the project root\n    24\t- **THEN** the skill reports \"Not a Node/TypeScript project\" and exits\n    25\t\n    26\t### Requirement: Detect oxlint config format\n    27\tThe skill SHALL detect the oxlint configuration file in any supported format.\n    28\t\n    29\t#### Scenario: Dotfile JSON config\n    30\t- **WHEN** `.oxlintrc.json` exists at the project root\n    31\t- **THEN** the skill reads and parses it as the active oxlint config\n    32\t\n    33\t#### Scenario: JSONC config\n    34\t- **WHEN** `.oxlintrc.jsonc` exists at the project root\n    35\t- **THEN** the skill reads it as the active oxlint config (strip comments before parsing)\n    36\t\n    37\t#### Scenario: TypeScript config\n    38\t- **WHEN** `oxlint.config.ts` exists at the project root\n    39\t- **THEN** the skill reports \"TypeScript oxlint config detected\" and notes that auto-apply is limited to manual instructions for this format\n    40\t\n    41\t#### Scenario: Multiple config formats\n    42\t- **WHEN** more than one oxlint config format exists in the same directory\n    43\t- **THEN** the skill warns \"Multiple oxlint config formats found\" and asks the user which to use\n    44\t\n    45\t#### Scenario: No config file\n    46\t- **WHEN** no oxlint config file exists\n    47\t- **THEN** the skill reports \"No oxlint config found (using defaults)\" and proceeds with audit\n    48\t\n    49\t### Requirement: Discover tsconfig files in monorepo\n    50\tThe skill SHALL find all TypeScript configuration files across the project.\n    51\t\n    52\t#### Scenario: Single tsconfig at root\n    53\t- **WHEN** only `tsconfig.json` exists at the project root\n    54\t- **THEN** the skill audits that single file\n    55\t\n    56\t#### Scenario: Monorepo with subproject tsconfigs\n    57\t- **WHEN** multiple `tsconfig*.json` files exist in subproject directories\n    58\t- **THEN** the skill discovers files up to depth 3 and reports each one separately. If deeper files are found, it warns \"Deeper tsconfig files may exist beyond depth 3\"\n    59\t\n    60\t#### Scenario: Extended tsconfig\n    61\t- **WHEN** a `tsconfig.json` uses `extends` to inherit from another config\n    62\t- **THEN** the skill resolves the effective config via `tsc --showConfig -p <tsconfig>` for strictness evaluation, so inherited settings are not falsely reported as missing\n    63\t\n    64\t#### Scenario: Named tsconfig variants\n    65\t- **WHEN** `tsconfig.app.json`, `tsconfig.node.json`, or other named variants exist\n    66\t- **THEN** the skill includes them in discovery alongside `tsconfig.json`\n    67\t\n    68\t### Requirement: Verify type-aware linting is enabled\n    69\tThe skill SHALL check that oxlint runs with type-aware mode.\n    70\t\n    71\t#### Scenario: Type-aware flag present\n    72\t- **WHEN** the project's lint script contains `--type-aware` or the oxlint config enables type-aware mode\n    73\t- **THEN** the skill reports type-aware linting as enabled\n    74\t\n    75\t#### Scenario: Type-aware flag missing\n    76\t- **WHEN** the project's lint script does not contain `--type-aware` and the config does not enable it\n    77\t- **THEN** the skill reports type-aware linting as missing and recommends adding `--type-aware` to the lint command\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
