{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_53","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"sed -n '1,260p' openspec/changes/lint-audit-skill/mission/validations/adversarial-a.md\"","aggregated_output":"# Adversarial Review A: lint-audit-skill\n\nBase SHA: `cdba772954cefd46dffc72713af62aa5ec072b19`\n\nCommits reviewed:\n\n- `e2ce827` `[task-1] Create rules.json agent-friendly ruleset`\n- `3057c01` `[task-2] Build audit.sh detection and comparison script`\n- `6e3929b` `[task-3] Write SKILL.md workflow and RULES.md documentation`\n- `d66eb18` `[task-4] Fix audit.sh for malformed tsconfig and rules-as-array`\n- `384089e` `[task-6] Add lint-audit to README skill table and ARCH.md`\n\n## Task 1: rules.json\n\n### MEDIUM: Import rules can be recommended without enabling the import plugin\n\n`skills/lint-audit/rules.json:89` and `skills/lint-audit/rules.json:96` include `import/no-cycle` and `import/named`, but the ruleset has no way to represent required plugin activation. `skills/lint-audit/audit.sh:229` only checks whether the rule key exists under `rules`; it does not check `plugins`. `skills/lint-audit/SKILL.md:160` and `skills/lint-audit/SKILL.md:174` also instruct the agent to create or update only the `rules` object.\n\nThe result is a likely false sense of enforcement: the audit can report an `import/*` rule as enabled, and the apply workflow can add it, without ensuring the `import` plugin is active. Oxlint's own `import/no-cycle` usage example includes `plugins: [\"import\"]` alongside the rule. At minimum, the ruleset needs plugin metadata or the apply workflow needs special handling for `import/*` rules.\n\n## Task 2: audit.sh\n\n### HIGH: Missing `tsc` causes required `migration_scope` fields to disappear\n\n`skills/lint-audit/audit.sh:288` checks only `command -v tsc`. If `tsc` is not globally installed, the script still reports `strict` and `noUncheckedIndexedAccess` as missing, but `skills/lint-audit/audit.sh:366` leaves `migration_scope_json=null`, and `skills/lint-audit/audit.sh:392` omits the field entirely.\n\nThis violates the contract items requiring an integer `migration_scope` for each missing `strict` and `noUncheckedIndexedAccess` setting. It also misses the common Node setup where TypeScript is installed at `node_modules/.bin/tsc`, not globally.\n\nRepro run in this workspace:\n\n```sh\nmkdir -p \"$tmp/project\"\nprintf '%s\\n' '{\"devDependencies\":{\"oxlint\":\"1.0.0\"}}' > \"$tmp/project/package.json\"\nprintf '%s\\n' '{\"compilerOptions\":{\"strict\":false,\"noUncheckedIndexedAccess\":false}}' > \"$tmp/project/tsconfig.json\"\nskills/lint-audit/audit.sh \"$tmp/project\" | jq '.tsconfig_findings'\n```\n\nObserved output has missing `strict` and `noUncheckedIndexedAccess` settings with no `migration_scope` fields, plus a separate `tsc` error finding.\n\n### MEDIUM: tsconfig discovery misses depth-3 subproject configs and never warns about deeper configs\n\n`openspec/changes/lint-audit-skill/specs/project-detection/spec.md:56` requires subproject tsconfigs up to depth 3 and a warning when deeper files may exist. `skills/lint-audit/audit.sh:280` uses:\n\n```sh\nfind . \\( -path './*/*/*/*' -prune \\) -o -type f -name 'tsconfig*.json' -print\n```\n\nThat prunes `./a/b/c/tsconfig.json` before the `-type f` check, so a tsconfig inside a depth-3 directory is omitted. There is also no warning channel or finding for deeper tsconfigs.\n\nRepro: a project containing only `a/b/c/tsconfig.json` returns `tsconfig_findings: []`.\n\n### MEDIUM: JSONC stripping corrupts valid strings containing comment markers\n\n`skills/lint-audit/audit.sh:123` strips comments with regexes over raw text. This corrupts valid JSONC strings that contain `//` or `/* */`, then reports an `oxlint-config` parse error instead of reading the config.\n\nRepro:\n\n```jsonc\n{\n  \"rules\": {\n    \"typescript/no-explicit-any\": \"error\"\n  },\n  \"note\": \"keep // inside string\"\n}\n```\n\n`skills/lint-audit/audit.sh` returns `outcome: \"ok\"` with an `oxlint-config` error finding: `Unfinished string at EOF`. The contract says a lone `.oxlintrc.jsonc` should be read and parsed after stripping comments.\n\n### MEDIUM: Explicitly disabled rules lose the rationale the spec requires\n\n`openspec/changes/lint-audit-skill/specs/ruleset-audit/spec.md:14` says explicitly disabled rules should include a note explaining why the rule matters for agents. `skills/lint-audit/audit.sh:242` only includes rationale when `$status == null` and the rule is not default-enabled. For a rule explicitly set to `\"off\"`, the output is:\n\n```json\n{\n  \"name\": \"typescript/no-non-null-assertion\",\n  \"status\": \"disabled\",\n  \"category\": \"type-safety\",\n  \"rationale\": \"\"\n}\n```\n\nThe committed test at `skills/lint-audit/tests/audit-sh.sh:132` checks only `status == \"disabled\"`, so it misses the empty rationale.\n\n## Task 3: SKILL.md and RULES.md\n\n### MEDIUM: Type-aware apply instruction can put `--type-aware` on the wrong command\n\n`skills/lint-audit/SKILL.md:212` tells the agent to append `--type-aware` to `scripts.lint`. That is unsafe for common compound scripts.\n\nExample:\n\n```json\n{\n  \"scripts\": {\n    \"lint\": \"oxlint . && tsc --noEmit\"\n  }\n}\n```\n\nAppending to the full string produces `oxlint . && tsc --noEmit --type-aware`, which passes the oxlint flag to `tsc`. The workflow should instruct the agent to modify the oxlint invocation specifically, or stop and ask when the lint script has multiple commands.\n\n### LOW: `oxlint.config.ts` detection does not produce actionable rule recommendations\n\n`skills/lint-audit/audit.sh:191` treats any TypeScript config as a parse error and emits a single `oxlint-config` error finding at `skills/lint-audit/audit.sh:203`. `skills/lint-audit/SKILL.md:183` says `.ts` configs should not be auto-edited and should receive manual rule JSON instructions, but there are no missing rule findings for the user to accept.\n\nThis technically detects the TypeScript config, but it does not support the apply workflow described by the skill. A safer output would mark the config as manual-only while still listing the rules that are missing or cannot be verified.\n\n## Task 4: Smoke test\n\n### HIGH: The 200-iteration validation artifact contradicts the contract\n\nThe contract requires a test harness running 200 unique scenarios and a final run with 0 failures. `openspec/changes/lint-audit-skill/mission/validations/smoke-test-200.md:3` says `PASS (197/200 real tests, 3 bugs found and fixed)`, not 200/200. `openspec/changes/lint-audit-skill/mission/validations/smoke-test-200.md:42` says tests 171-180 initially failed due to harness escaping, and `openspec/changes/lint-audit-skill/mission/validations/smoke-test-200.md:43` says test 99 was excluded.\n\nNo durable harness was committed, so the 200 scenarios cannot be re-run from the repository. This looks like validation coverage was summarized rather than preserved, and the summary does not prove the task's required final state.\n\n## Task 5: Cleanup\n\nNo additional findings.\n\n`shellcheck` is not installed in this environment, so I could not run it. `sh -n` passed for:\n\n- `skills/lint-audit/audit.sh`\n- `skills/lint-audit/tests/audit-sh.sh`\n- `skills/lint-audit/tests/validate-rules-json.sh`\n\n## Task 6: install.sh, README.md, ARCH.md\n\nNo findings.\n\n`install.sh` already symlinks every directory under `skills/`, so the new `skills/lint-audit` directory is covered without a dedicated install.sh diff. README.md and ARCH.md both mention lint-audit.\n\n## Task 7: Module shape re-check\n\n### LOW: Module-shape validation misses output-shape drift around optional fields\n\n`openspec/changes/lint-audit-skill/mission/validations/module-shape-audit.md:12` says `AuditReport` matches the design, and line 13 accepts optional fields. The real output shape has contract-significant omissions: `migration_scope` disappears when `tsc` is unavailable, and `severity` is not carried into oxlint findings even though `SKILL.md:158` expects it when present.\n\nThis is lower severity than the Task 2 bug itself, but it shows the module-shape audit did not check the consumer-facing JSON contract deeply enough.\n\n## Cross-task pass\n\nThe main cross-task mismatch is that `rules.json`, `audit.sh`, and `SKILL.md` do not carry enough metadata for the apply workflow to produce enforceable config in every case:\n\n- `rules.json` knows rule names and severity, but not plugin prerequisites.\n- `audit.sh` reports whether a rule key is present, but not whether prerequisites like `plugins: [\"import\"]` are present.\n- `SKILL.md` applies accepted rules by adding only `rules`, so a re-run can look clean while the underlying linter may still not enforce the intended rule.\n\nThe second cross-task mismatch is that the script's JSON output is looser than the contract and skill workflow assume:\n\n- Missing TypeScript settings may not include `migration_scope`.\n- Disabled rules may not include rationale.\n- TypeScript oxlint configs produce an error finding rather than manual rule recommendations.\n\n## Verification performed\n\nCommands run:\n\n- `git log --reverse --format='%h %s' cdba772954cefd46dffc72713af62aa5ec072b19..HEAD`\n- `git show --stat --oneline --decorate <sha>` for all 5 commits\n- `git show --format=fuller --patch d66eb18`\n- `skills/lint-audit/tests/validate-rules-json.sh skills/lint-audit/rules.json`\n- `skills/lint-audit/tests/audit-sh.sh`\n- `sh -n skills/lint-audit/audit.sh`\n- `sh -n skills/lint-audit/tests/audit-sh.sh`\n- `sh -n skills/lint-audit/tests/validate-rules-json.sh`\n- Manual repros for missing `migration_scope`, depth-3 tsconfig discovery, JSONC string handling, and disabled-rule rationale.\n\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
